# Python review
Check mutable defaults, shared mutable state, iterator consumption, None/empty paths,
context-manager cleanup, swallowed exceptions and awaited async operations.
Distinguish truthiness from explicit None checks only where the difference causes visible behavior.
Do not assume input types, framework injection or coroutine scheduling not present in the supplied lines.

For Python boolean expressions, track the returned VALUE and TYPE, not only its truthiness.
`x or y` returns x if x is truthy, otherwise y EXACTLY, even when y is an empty string,
False, zero or None. It never automatically converts a falsy last operand to None.
`x and y` returns x if x is falsy, otherwise y exactly. `is None` matches only None;
False, zero and empty strings do not take an `is None` branch. Follow only the branch
selected by the concrete value. An explicit False return remains False through a function call.
Compare the resulting value with the required outcome; matching outcomes are not defects.

Value checks for short-circuit expressions (language semantics, not findings):
- `None or ""` evaluates to ""; `"" or None` evaluates to None; `"" or ""` evaluates to "".
- A function `return default if value is None else value` preserves "", False, and 0.
- A chain `a or b or original` preserves original when both a and b are falsey.
Evaluate operand order as written, including the last operand. No backward jump or implicit cast.
A repair must preserve explicit caller settings as well as the demonstrated boundary; do not
replace an original True/False setting with a default or None without checking the caller contract.
